title: Merge-safe numbering across branches
effort: M
area: format
created: 2026-09-17T04:20:03Z
updated: 2026-09-17T04:39:14Z

Sequential numbers collided when two branches allocated at once: a branch
creating .pidly/5 and another creating .pidly/7/5 merged cleanly and left two
tickets numbered 5. The lock could not help — branches do not see each other
until merge.

Allocation now also writes .pidly/.n/<number>, holding a nonce and the title,
never modified afterwards, and committed. Two branches taking the same number
write the same path with different content, so git stops with an add/add
conflict naming the number. Different numbers merge cleanly.

This detects rather than prevents. Only a non-coordinating id — hash or
timestamp — prevents it, and that costs the readable number the format is
built around. Being told at merge time, before anything is built on the bad
state, was the trade taken.

Two things came free: a number is never reissued even against a hand-deleted
directory, and a dangling needs has something to say about what the number
was. The registry can lag, since creating a ticket with mkdir is allowed, so
the next number still considers the tree and init backfills what it finds.

Done: implemented in bin/pidly, covered by tests/merge.sh (8 cases driving
real git merges) and additions to tests/init.sh.
